1063 #edb/ref 17592186060868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060829 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060829 #edb/ref 1061 5325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060829 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "469e26a7-ca8d-5fb8-91ed-400f5368f8de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060829 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060830 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060830 #edb/ref 1061 5328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060830 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f70e43c-c28a-5ff3-817c-23c9e9a9a7e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060830 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186060869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060831 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186060832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060831 #edb/ref 1061 5329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060831 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e56edb46-4172-5ede-aeb1-40c6e064488c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060831 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059858 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060832 #edb/ref 1061 5332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060832 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "856610ea-9f57-51a1-9df2-7afa99daa958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060832 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060868 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use the standard algorithm to multiply two-digit numbers.\n\nTo illustrate, let's use the standard algorithm to compute $12\\times 13$.\n\nRecall that ${\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}3}$ in expanded form is ${\\color{blue}10} + {\\color{red}3}$. The idea is to first multiply $12$ by ${\\color{red}3}$, then by ${\\color{blue}10}$, and add the results.\n\nWe write one number above the other as follows:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}36 + 120 = 156\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the top number ($12$) by the value in the ones place of the second number ($\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{3}$), writing the result under the line:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3; 3 \\mid 6 \\mid 12 \\cdot 3 = 36000\n$$\n\nNext, we multiply $12$ by $\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1}0$. Let's do this in steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Any number multiplied by $10$ always gives a zero at the end. Therefore, we place a zero in the ones column underneath our first result.\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3; 3\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}12 \\cdot 10 = 1200\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Now, we work out $\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2}\\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1}$. We place the answer next to our zero:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3; 3\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 2 \\cdot 1 = 20\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Next, we we work out $\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1}\\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{1}$:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3; 3\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ 1\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 0 \\mid 1 \\cdot 1 = 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n1\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}3; 3\\begin{vmatrix}6 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}6 \\mid 36 + 120 = 156\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n12 \\times 13 = 156\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060868 #edb/ref 1065 1758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060868 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c5e81bce-1ac7-5040-8119-b2548f14f16f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060868 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060869 #edb/ref 1064 "Just like when multiplying a number by a one-digit number, we will sometimes have carries. Let's see how to deal with this by multiplying $24$ by $32$.\n\nWe write it this way:\n\n$$\n2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}48 + 720 = 768\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the ones:\n\n$$\n0; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; 4 \\mid 8 \\mid 24 \\cdot 2 = 48000\n$$\n\nOn this occasion, there were no carries when multiplying the ones, and so the carry box $0$ remains empty.\n\nNext, we multiply the tens. Let's do this in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: We start by writing a zero:\n\n$$\n0; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}24 \\cdot 30 = 7200\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: We now work out\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{4}\\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{3} = {\\color{blue}1}{\\color{red}2}\n$$\n\nWe carry the ${\\color{blue}1}$ and write down $\\color{red}2$:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ 2\\end{vmatrix}0 \\mid 4 \\cdot 3 = 12; Carry:1; Write:2\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We multiply\n\n$$\n\\bbox[2px, lightgray]{2}\\times \\bbox[2px, lightgray]{3} = 6\n$$\n\nand we add the carry from the previous multiplication to make\n\n$$\n6+{\\color{blue}{1}} = {\\color{red}{7}}\n$$\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ 7\\end{vmatrix}2 \\mid 0 \\mid 2 \\cdot 3 = 6and6 + 1 = 7; Carry: -; Write:7\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the results:\n\n$$\n[math]\\color{blue}1[/math]; [math]\\color{blue}\\phantom{0}[/math]; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\\\ \\cdot\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}2; 4\\begin{vmatrix}8 \\\\ +\\end{vmatrix}7\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}8 \\mid 48 + 720 = 768\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n24 \\times 32 = 768\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060869 #edb/ref 1065 1759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060869 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7fed308-698f-538a-ba9e-c52f4af74118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060869 #edb/ref 1067 "Multiplying Tw